AlexO <alexolog@gmail.com> wrote:
> While I agree with most of Patric said, I believe the issue involves
> more than a defunct DevTeam.
>
> The problem, as I see it, is that there is nobody who is (a) willing
> to take over and (b) unify the community behind him (or her).

For (a) you need someone who wants to do it. But that is always the
necessary precondition for something to happen. Somebody must step
forward and do it.

I have my doubts about (b). NetHack's community is not really well
defined and I wouldn't even say that it is unified at the moment.

> True, on one hand we have the DevTeam being, excuse my vernacular,
> dicks by not releasing bugfixes (or even the bug reports) for more
> than 7 years; but on the other hand we have *two* variants in active
> development, neither of which claims to be the next incarnation (well,
> it could be worse, they could both be fighting over the title).

Ha! UnNetHack would clearly win! It's Levenshtein distance to NetHack
is much better than SporkHack's!

;-)

> So as long as there two variants which are quite similar and neither
> clearly superior, the community will remain fractured and none of the
> variants will gain the necessary traction.
>
> Let's say I find a bug in Vanilla.  Do I spend time to reproduce and
> document it?  Who shall I send the report to?  The DevTeam, and have
> it disappear into Gehennom for another 7 years?  To the Spork or to
> the Un devs?  What if it no longer there, or is there but cannot be
> reproduced in the same way because it's hidden by code changes?  Or do
> I just say to hell with it?

If it's a bug in SporkHack or UnNetHack, report it to the appropriate
DevTeam. We are in contact, any bug that also affects the other
variant will get noticed and fixed.

We usually also check if Vanilla NetHack is affected.

But you also have to know that Derek is not really interested in
fixing non-critical vanilla bugs. Anything that crashes SporkHack will
be fixed but everything else is not a given.


If you find a bug in vanilla NetHack, it is not that clear what to do,
as you said yourself.

I think the best approach is to post it in here. The DevTeam's buglist
is not really easy to parse (for some bugs nobody outside the devteam
knows really what the bug descriptions really mean), but the group
is usually able to say if it's a known bug.

If not you can still always ask the DevTeam directly. They might
answer.

> Patric mentioned Crawl.  To which I can reply by saying that it worked
> largely due to the fact that Stone Soup was the only viable variant.

I disagree. For a time, Slash'Em was rather popular not because it was
alone but because it provided a gaming experience unlike NetHack.

> Unfortunately, neither Derek nor Patric showed any inclination to work
> toward that goal
[taking a variant and calling it "NetHack"]

Derek would have the balls to do it but he doesn't have the time to do
it and he also thinks he would have to fix most vanilla bugs first.

I would have the balls, too (hey, stop snickering!), but I don't think
that it would make sense to do. 

I don't think that the DevTeam would magically appear and be active
again. IMO the more probably scenario would be that a lot of vanilla
players would hassle the impostor. You would have to fight and discuss
a lot for not much gain. That's not something I want to do for
something that should be fun.

> so my guess is that there will never be any new
> "official" version and the community will remain split among similar-
> but-somewhat-different versions with some preferring Spork, others
> preferring Un and yet others sticking with Vanilla and hoping the
> DevTeam's second coming.

I don't have a problem that people play the game(s) that they like
most.

If they have several nethackish games to choose from that just means
more power to the players.

